.SH DESCRIPTION
.B helikodu\-cli
is the command-line tool of Helikodu.
It plays files in the terminal, lists the audio devices, and scans and
queries the same library the desktop application uses.
These commands work on their own; the application need not be running.
The commands under
.B COMMANDS FOR THE RUNNING APPLICATION
control the running application over D-Bus instead.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS play
Plays the files in the order given.
Files of the same format join without a gap.
A file that cannot be opened or decoded is reported and skipped.
.TP
.BI \-\-backend " backend"
.B auto
(the default: PipeWire if it is running, otherwise ALSA),
.BR pipewire ,
.BR alsa ,
.B null
(discards the audio) or
.BR wav .
.TP
.BI \-\-device " id"
The device's stable name, as
.B list\-outputs
shows it: a PipeWire node name such as
.BR alsa_output.usb\-Topping_D10s\-00.analog\-stereo ,
or an ALSA PCM such as
.BR hw:CARD=D10s,DEV=0 .
The default is the backend's default device.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " mode"
.B shared
(the default) or
.BR exclusive .
.TP
.BI \-\-volume " n"
The position of the volume control, 0 to 100 (the default).
It follows the cubic curve of desktop mixers.
.TP
.BI \-\-wav\-out " path"
The file the
.B wav
backend writes; required with
.B \-\-backend wav
and refused otherwise.
The file is written as fast as the input decodes.
.TP
.BI \-\-replaygain " mode"
Loudness normalization by ReplayGain tags or Opus R128 tags:
.B off
(the default),
.BR track ,
.B album
or
.B auto
(the album gain when a neighbouring file in the list has the same album
title and album artists, the track gain otherwise).
A file without the chosen gain uses the other one; a file without either
plays unchanged.
A positive gain is lowered so that the tagged peak stays at or below full scale.
.TP
.BI \-\-preamp " dB"
Added to the gain of files with loudness tags, \-24 to 24 (default 0).
.TP
.B \-\-dsp
Processes the audio with the DSP chain of the settings file (its
.B [dsp]
table: the equalizer, convolution, the compressor, pitch shift and the
other stages, and the playback speed).
With
.B \-\-backend wav
the processed audio is written to the file.
.PP
When standard input is a terminal, these keys control playback and a status
line on standard error shows the file, position, volume and output:
.TP
.B Space
Pause or resume.
.TP
.BR n ", " p
Next file; previous file (restarts the current file when more than three
seconds of it have played).
.TP
.BR Left ", " Right
Seek back or forward 5 seconds.
.TP
.BR + ", " \-
Volume up or down by 5.
.TP
.B o
Move playback to the next output device, through the PipeWire and then the
ALSA devices, without stopping.
.TP
.BR q ", " Esc ", " Ctrl+C
Quit.
.PP
Otherwise the files play to the end without reading input, and a lost device
ends playback.
.B play
writes messages to standard error and nothing to standard output.
.SS list\-outputs
Lists the devices of every backend that plays sound (PipeWire and ALSA), or
of the one given with
.BR \-\-backend
.RB ( auto ,
.BR pipewire ,
.B alsa
or
.BR null ).
The default device is marked with
.BR * .
With
.B \-\-json
the output is a JSON array with an object per device:
.BR backend ,
.BR id ,
.BR description ,
.B is_default
and
.B capabilities
.RB ( sample_rates ,
.BR formats ,
.BR channel_counts ).
.SS scan
Adds the folders to the library and reads their new and changed files;
files that disappeared are marked missing.
Without folders, every folder already in the library is scanned again.
Progress goes to standard error, the summary to standard output.
.TP
.BI \-\-exclude " pattern"
Leaves out every file and folder whose name matches the pattern
.RB ( *
and
.B ?
are wildcards; case matters), or, when the pattern contains a
.BR / ,
the file or folder at that path.
May be given more than once.
.TP
.BI \-\-workers " n"
The number of worker processes, 1 to 64.
The default is one per processor, at most 8.
.TP
.BI \-\-worker " path"
The scan worker program.
The default is
.B helikodu\-scan\-worker
beside
.B helikodu\-cli
or in
.I ../libexec/helikodu/
from it.
.SS search
Finds the tracks whose title, artists, album, album artists, genres,
composers or path contain every word of
.IR text ,
ignoring case, accents and character width, best matches first.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " n"
At most
.I n
results, 1 to 1000 (default 50).
.TP
.BI \-\-offset " n"
Skips the first
.I n
results (default 0).
.SS info
Shows the tags, audio properties and loudness values of a file, read
directly, with its library entry and statistics when it is in the library.
An argument that is not an existing file is taken as a track number, as
.B search
prints it, and the track is shown from the library.
.SS stats
Shows the library folders, the totals and the most played tracks.
.TP
.BI \-\-top " n"
How many of the most played tracks to show, 0 to 1000 (default 10).
.SS loudness
Measures the loudness of library tracks (EBU R128, ITU-R BS.1770-4):
integrated loudness, loudness range, sample peak and true peak, and the
ReplayGain 2.0 gain relative to \-18 LUFS.
The tracks are given as files in the library or as track numbers.
The results are stored in the library, where playback prefers them over the
values in the tags.
Progress goes to standard error, the results to standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-album
Measures the whole albums of the tracks given, with the album gain: the
album measured as one programme.
A track given without this option gets track values only.
.TP
.B \-\-library
Measures every library track that has no current measurement (never
measured, or its file changed since), with the other tracks of its album.
Takes no tracks.
.TP
.B \-\-write\-tags
Also writes the values into the files' tags (REPLAYGAIN_TRACK_GAIN,
REPLAYGAIN_TRACK_PEAK and the album pair).
Tracks of CUE sheets are not written.
.TP
.BI \-\-threads " n"
The number of measuring threads, 1 to 64.
The default is half the processors.
.TP
.BI \-\-worker " path"
The scan worker program that reads written files again, as for
.BR scan .
.SS analyze
Analyses every library track that has no current analysis, one at a time,
as the application does in the background: where its sound starts and ends,
its silent stretches, its loudness envelope and its sound features.
The same pass measures the loudness of tracks without any loudness values
(no tags, no measurement), an album as one programme.
Each track's result is stored at once; a file that cannot be decoded is
recorded and not tried again until it changes.
Progress goes to standard error.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-loudness
Leaves the loudness measurement out.
.SS similar
Lists the library tracks that sound most like a track, given as a library
file or a track number, from the sound features of the track analysis: most
alike first, with the track number, the similarity from 0 to 1, artist,
title, album and length.
Copies of the same recording are left out.
The track must have been analysed (see
.BR analyze ).
.TP
.B \-\-album
Compares with the album of the track as a whole and leaves out the album's
own tracks.
.TP
.B \-\-artist
The same for the first artist of the track.
.TP
.BI \-\-limit " n"
At most
.I n
tracks (default 20).
.SS duplicates
Lists the groups of copies in the library: files with exactly the same
content.
Each group shows its main copy, the other copies, and files that are the same
file reached by another path (such as a hard link); missing files are
marked.
A summary line gives the number of groups and the size of the copies besides
the main copies.
Nothing is moved or deleted; the application's Find Duplicates window does
that.
.SS import
Imports play counts, ratings and playlists from another player:
.BR rhythmbox ,
.BR strawberry ,
.BR clementine ,
.B lollypop
or
.B itunes
(an exported Library.xml).
The player's files are only read; its databases are read from a copy.
Without
.B \-\-path
the file is looked for where the player keeps it, Flatpak folders included.
Entries are matched to library tracks by path, then by artist, title, album,
track number and a length within 2 seconds.
Without
.B \-\-apply
the command only shows what would change.
Play and skip counts are added (importing the same file again adds only the
plays made since); ratings are taken for unrated tracks only; the earliest
first and the latest last play win.
.TP
.BI \-\-path " path"
The player's library file, or the folder holding it.
.TP
.BI \-\-map " from" = to
A path prefix of the other library and the folder it is now.
A prefix with a drive letter is matched without regard to case.
May be given several times.
.TP
.B \-\-replace\-ratings
Takes the imported rating also for tracks that have one.
.TP
.B \-\-no\-playlists
Leaves the playlists out.
.TP
.B \-\-apply
Carries out the import, in one transaction.
.SS undo\-import
Undoes the newest import or carried statistics: counts lose what it added,
other values go back where they still hold what it set, and the playlists it
made are deleted.
.B \-\-list
lists the imports instead.
.SS export\-user\-data
Writes the ratings, play counts, history, playlists, smart playlists,
Favourites, saved searches, labels and fields to a JSON file, keyed by path and tags.
.SS import\-user\-data
Reads a file written by
.B export\-user\-data
into the library, with the options of
.BR import .
Counts take the higher value, so importing a file twice changes nothing.
.SS carry\-stats
Lists the missing files whose statistics, history and playlist entries a file
of the same recording (the same title and artist, a length within 2 seconds)
could take over.
With
.BR \-\-apply ,
carries them where there is one replacement; with
.B \-\-from
and
.BR \-\-to ,
carries them between the two files named.
.SS write\-stats
Writes the play counts and ratings that changed since they were last written
into the files' tags, as the
.B write_ratings_to_tags
and
.B write_play_counts_to_tags
settings say, or as
.B \-\-ratings
and
.B \-\-play\-counts
say.
.SS managed\-folder
The managed media folder, the
.B [library.managed]
table of the settings file.
Change it while the application is closed, or the application may write its
own copy of the settings over it.
.B managed\-folder show
prints the folder, its naming pattern and mode, and a run that was cut short.
.B managed\-folder set
.RI [ folder ]
turns it on or changes it; a folder outside every library folder becomes a
library folder:
.TP
.BI \-\-pattern " pattern"
The naming pattern below the folder, as in the file organizer.
.TP
.BI \-\-mode " mode"
Whether imports
.B copy
or
.B move
the files.
.TP
.BI \-\-characters " set"
Which characters names may hold:
.B portable
(names Windows can read),
.B as\-is
or
.BR ascii .
.PP
.B managed\-folder off
turns it off; its files stay where they are.
.BI "managed\-folder import " path ...
copies or moves files and folders from outside the library into the managed
folder, named by its pattern, with their covers, CUE sheets, lyrics and logs.
.B managed\-folder consolidate
moves or copies the library files outside the managed folder into it; the
tracks keep their ratings, play counts and history.
Both take:
.TP
.BR \-\-move ", " \-\-copy
Moves or copies the files, whatever the setting says.
.TP
.B \-\-dry\-run
Only shows the plan: every file and where it goes, names that are taken,
skipped files, and the space needed and free.
Nothing changes.
.PP
.B managed\-folder resume
continues a run that was cut short, and
.B managed\-folder roll\-back
rolls it back: copies go, moved files go back.
No new run starts before one of them.
.SS labels
Labels are free words given to library tracks, albums and artists.
They are kept in the library and never written to the files; a label of an
album or an artist counts for all of its tracks.
.B labels list
lists them with how many tracks, albums and artists have them;
.B labels show
the labels of what it names.
.BI "labels add " label
and
.BI "labels remove " label
give and take a label;
.BI "labels rename " "label new-name" ,
.BI "labels merge " "from into"
and
.BI "labels delete " label
change the labels themselves.
.BR show ,
.B add
and
.B remove
take exactly one of:
.TP
.BI \-\-track " id" ...
Library tracks, by the ids
.B search
prints.
.TP
.BI \-\-query " query"
The tracks a query of the application's search language finds.
.TP
.BI \-\-album\-of " track-id"
The album of a library track.
.TP
.BI \-\-artist " name"
An artist, by name.
.SS fields
Fields the user defines, usable in searches and naming patterns.
.BI "fields define " "name expression"
defines a computed field, whose value is the expression's text, such as
.BR "fields define decade '{left(year,3)}0s'" ;
.BI "fields define " name " \-\-tag " tag
a custom field for a tag, such as
.BR "fields define mood \-\-tag MOOD" .
A definition that cannot be used is refused with the place of the problem
marked.
.B fields list
lists the fields,
.BI "fields remove " name
removes one, and
.BI "fields value " "name track-id" ...
shows a field's value for library tracks.
The running application uses changed fields after it is started again.
.SS dsp
Shows and changes the DSP chain in the
.B [dsp]
table of the settings file; the running application reads a changed file the
next time it starts.
The stages are
.BR pitch ,
.BR equalizer ,
.BR convolution ,
.BR crossfeed ,
.BR channel\-mix ,
.B compressor
and
.BR limiter .
.TP
.B dsp show
Shows the stages in order, which are on, and the saved chains;
.B \-\-json
prints them as JSON.
.TP
.BI "dsp order " stage ...
Puts the stages in this order, first to last; stages left out follow in the
default order.
.B dsp default\-order
puts them back in the default order.
.TP
.BI "dsp on " stage "...\fR, \fBdsp off " stage ...
Switches stages on or off.
.TP
.B dsp chain list
Lists the saved chains.
.BI "dsp chain save " name
saves the current order and the stages that are on as a chain (a chain of
that name is replaced);
.BI "dsp chain apply " name
uses a saved chain: its stages in its order, the others off;
.BI "dsp chain delete " name
deletes one.
.TP
.BR "dsp compressor " [ night | gentle | custom ]
Switches the compressor on with a preset:
.B night
(the default: strong compression with makeup gain, for listening at a low
volume),
.B gentle
(light compression) or
.B custom
(the values of
.B [dsp.compressor]
in the settings file).
.TP
.BI "dsp convolution folder " folder
Uses the WAV files in a folder, one per sample rate, and switches
convolution on.
.BI "dsp convolution file " "rate path"
uses a WAV file for one sample rate in Hz;
.B dsp convolution clear
forgets the folder and the files and switches convolution off.
.TP
.B dsp convolution mix
Sets the mix:
.BI \-\-wet " percent"
the share of the processed signal, 0 to 100;
.BI \-\-gain " dB"
its gain, \-24 to 12;
.BR \-\-normalize " " true | false
whether the impulse response is scaled to boost no frequency.
.TP
.BR "dsp convolution check " [\fIfile\fR...]
Shows the impulse response the settings give for the common sample rates, or
describes WAV files: their rate, channels, length and the latency they add.
.TP
.BI "dsp pitch " semitones
Shifts the pitch by \-12 to 12 semitones, keeping the tempo; fractions are
cents.
.B 0
switches pitch shift off.
.TP
.BI "dsp speed " rate
The playback speed the application starts with, 0.5 to 2;
.B 1
switches it off.
.B \-\-keep\-pitch
keeps the pitch, so the tempo changes;
.B \-\-tape
lets the pitch follow the speed, as on a tape.
Without either, the pitch does as it was set before (kept by default).
The speed is not a stage of the order and is not kept in saved chains.
.TP
.B dsp track\-eq list
Lists the library tracks that have an equalizer of their own; it replaces
the equalizer of the settings while the track plays.
.BI "dsp track\-eq show " track-id
prints the equalizer of a track as JSON;
.BI "dsp track\-eq clear " track-id
removes it, so the track plays with the settings' equalizer again.
.TP
.BI "dsp track\-eq set " track-id
Gives a track an equalizer of its own, with one of:
.B \-\-from\-settings
(a copy of the equalizer of the settings),
.BI \-\-autoeq " file"
(an AutoEQ or EqualizerAPO ParametricEQ.txt file),
.BI \-\-preset " id"
(a built-in graphic preset such as
.BR flat ,
.B bass\-boost
or
.BR vocal )
or
.B \-\-none
(no equalizer for this track, whatever the settings say).
A change applies the next time the track starts.
.SS related
Lists the library tracks endless play would add after a library track, album
or artist, given by its number with
.BR \-\-track ,
.B \-\-album
or
.BR \-\-artist ,
with the
.B [playback.endless]
settings.
.TP
.BI \-\-count " n"
How many tracks to pick (default 20).
.TP
.BI \-\-random\-seed " n"
The random seed; the same seed picks the same tracks.
The default is taken from the time.
.SS auto\-dj
Lists the library tracks the Auto-DJ would add, with the
.B [playback.auto_dj]
settings.
.TP
.BI \-\-count " n"
How many tracks to pick; the default is
.BR playback.auto_dj.ahead .
.TP
.BI \-\-random\-seed " n"
The random seed, as for
.BR related .
.SS generator
Generator playlists are made by steps, one per line: a source
.RB ( "from library" ,
.BI "from playlist \(dq" name \(dq
or
.BI "from query " query\fR),
then filters
.RB ( where
.IR query ,
.B exclude playlist
.BI \(dq name \(dq\fR),
sorts
.RB ( sort
.I key
.RB [ asc | desc ],
by
.BR rating ,
.BR plays ,
.BR skips ,
.BR last\-played ,
.BR added ,
.BR year ,
.BR title ,
.BR artist ,
.BR album ,
.B length
or
.BR random ),
caps
.RB ( per\-artist
.IR n ,
.B per\-album
.IR n )
and a limit
.RB ( limit
.IR n ,
or
.B limit
.I n
.BR minutes ).
Lines starting with
.B #
are comments.
A generator is given by its name or its number, as
.B generator list
prints them.
.TP
.B generator list
Lists the generator playlists;
.BI "generator show " generator
prints its steps, in the form
.B add
and
.B edit
take.
.TP
.BI "generator add " name
Adds a generator with the steps of
.BI \-\-steps " text"
or
.BI \-\-steps\-file " file"
.RB ( \-
for the standard input).
It is made again each time it is opened, unless
.B \-\-keep
keeps the result until
.B run
makes it again.
.TP
.BI "generator edit " generator
Changes its name
.RB ( \-\-name
.IR name ),
its steps
.RB ( \-\-steps ,
.BR \-\-steps\-file )
or whether it is made again on open
.RB ( \-\-on\-open
.BR yes | no ).
.BI "generator remove " generator
removes it.
.TP
.BI "generator run " generator
Makes its tracks again, stores and prints them.
.BI "generator open " generator
prints the tracks as opening it shows them.
.B generator preview
runs the steps of
.B \-\-steps
or
.B \-\-steps\-file
without storing anything.
.B run
and
.B preview
take
.BI \-\-random\-seed " n"
for
.BR "sort random" .
.TP
.BI "generator play " generator
Opens the generator and plays its tracks in the running application,
replacing its queue, as the commands for the running application do.
.SH COMMANDS FOR THE RUNNING APPLICATION
These commands talk to the running Helikodu window through its D-Bus
interface,
.BR io.github.sorrelfox.Helikodu.Control1 .
They never start the application: when it is not running they say so and
end with exit status 4.
Playback asked for this way passes the same full-volume confirmation as in
the window.
.SS status
Shows the state, the playing track, the position, the volume, the play modes,
the place in the queue and the output.
.SS queue
Lists the queue in playing order; the current track is marked with
.BR > .
.SS playlists
Lists the playlists with their numbers, kinds and number of tracks.
.SS play\-pause, pause, resume, stop, next, previous
The transport.
.B previous
goes to the start of the track after its first seconds.
.SS seek
Moves to a
.I position
in the playing track, given in seconds
.RB ( 83 )
or as minutes and seconds
.RB ( 1:23 );
with
.B +
or
.B \-
in front it moves by that much
.RB ( +10 ", " \-5 ).
.SS volume
Without an argument prints the volume, 0 to 100.
With a
.IR level ,
0 to 100, sets it;
.B +5
and
.B \-5
change it by that much.
.SS shuffle
Sets the shuffle
.IR mode :
.BR off ", " tracks ", " albums ", " folders ", " groupings " or " within\-album .
.SS repeat
Sets the repeat
.IR mode :
.BR off ", " one " (the track), " album ", " all " (the queue),"
.B endless
(tracks related to the last one are added when the queue runs out, with the
.B [playback.endless]
settings) or
.B auto\-dj
(tracks from the library are kept queued ahead, with the
.B [playback.auto_dj]
settings).
.SS enqueue
Adds files to the end of the queue, or with
.B \-\-next
after the playing track, and prints how many were added.
.TP
.BI \-\-query " query"
Adds the library tracks the query finds instead of files (at most 10,000).
The query is written as in the search field of the application, for example
.BR "genre:jazz rating>=4" ;
text without fields searches for its words.
.TP
.B \-\-next
Adds after the playing track instead of at the end.
.SS play\-query
Replaces the queue with the library tracks the query finds (the words given
are joined into one query) and plays them.
Finding none is an error and leaves the queue as it is.
.TP
.B \-\-shuffle
Plays them in random order.
.SS rate
Rates the playing library track with 0 to 5
.IR stars ,
or removes its rating with
.BR none .
.TP
.BI \-\-track " id"
Rates this library track, as
.B search
prints it, instead.
.SS export\-playlist
Writes a playlist, given by its name or its number as
.B playlists
prints them, to
.IR file ;
the extension picks the format:
.BR .m3u8 ", " .m3u ", " .pls " or " .xspf .
.SS revert
Goes back to the track
.B next
last moved on from, where it was.
.SS radio\-random
Plays a random track of the queue from a random point in it.
.SS intro\-scan
Turns intro scan
.B on
or
.BR off :
only the first seconds of each track play, as many as the
.B playback.intro_scan_s
setting says (10 by default).
.SS start\-radio
Replaces the queue with a library track, album or artist, given by its number
with
.BR \-\-track ,
.B \-\-album
or
.BR \-\-artist ,
and related tracks, and turns on endless play, which keeps adding tracks
related to it.
.SS speed
Plays at another
.IR rate ,
0.5 to 2
.RB ( 1
is the normal speed), until the application ends; playback goes on where it
was.
.B dsp speed
sets the speed it starts with.
.TP
.B \-\-keep\-pitch
Keeps the pitch, so the tempo changes (the default).
.TP
.B \-\-tape
Lets the pitch follow the speed, as on a tape.
.SH COMMON OPTIONS
.TP
.B \-\-json
Prints the result as JSON.
Paths are given as text; a path that is not valid UTF-8 also has
.BR path_bytes ,
its exact bytes.
Times are seconds since the Unix epoch, lengths are milliseconds, and unknown
values are
.BR null .
Later versions may add keys; existing keys keep their meaning.
.TP
.BI \-\-database " path"
Uses this library database instead of the default one.
.TP
.BI \-\-settings " path"
Uses this settings file instead of the default one
.RB ( managed\-folder
and
.BR dsp ).
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Prints help for the tool or a command.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Prints the version.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success.
For
.BR play ,
at least one file played; for
.BR scan ,
files that could not be read do not change the status;
.B search
without a match is a success.
.TP
.B 1
A runtime error: the output could not be opened or failed, no backend could
be listed, there is no library yet, the library or a file could not be read,
a scan could not start, or
.B info
found no such track; for
.BR loudness ,
also a track that could not be measured or whose tags could not be written;
for
.BR analyze ,
a track that could not be analysed; for
.BR similar ,
no similar track (the track is not analysed yet or is silent); for
.BR managed\-folder ,
a file that could not be placed or a run that was not finished.
.TP
.B 2
A usage error: an unknown option or value, or options that do not go together.
.TP
.B 3
.B play
could not play any of the files.
.TP
.B 4
A command for the running application found it not running, or found no
session bus.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B XDG_DATA_HOME
The library database is
.I $XDG_DATA_HOME/helikodu/library.db
.RI ( ~/.local/share/helikodu/library.db
when it is not set).
.TP
.B XDG_CONFIG_HOME
The settings file is
.I $XDG_CONFIG_HOME/helikodu/settings.toml
.RI ( ~/.config/helikodu/settings.toml
when it is not set).
.TP
.BR LC_ALL ", " LC_COLLATE ", " LANG
The locale that sorting follows.
.TP
.B DBUS_SESSION_BUS_ADDRESS
The session bus the commands for the running application use.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I ~/.local/share/helikodu/library.db
The library database, shared with
.BR helikodu (1).
.B scan
creates it; the other library commands need it, except
.B info
on a file.
.TP
.I ~/.config/helikodu/settings.toml
The settings file, shared with
.BR helikodu (1).
.BR dsp ,
.BR managed\-folder ,
.BR related ,
.B auto\-dj
and
.B play \-\-dsp
read it;
.B dsp
and
.B managed\-folder
also write it.
.SH EXAMPLES
List the ALSA devices, then play an album exclusively to one of them with
album gain:
.PP
.RS
.EX
helikodu\-cli list\-outputs \-\-backend alsa
helikodu\-cli play ~/Music/Album/*.flac \-\-backend alsa \e
    \-\-device \(aqhw:CARD=D10s,DEV=0\(aq \-\-mode exclusive \-\-replaygain album
.EE
.RE
.PP
Scan a folder and search it:
.PP
.RS
.EX
helikodu\-cli scan ~/Music \-\-exclude \(aq*.part\(aq
helikodu\-cli search miles davis \-\-limit 10 \-\-json
.EE
.RE
Play the best rated jazz of the library in the running application,
shuffled, then skip a track:
.PP
.RS
.EX
helikodu\-cli play\-query \-\-shuffle \(aqgenre:jazz rating>=4\(aq
helikodu\-cli next
helikodu\-cli status \-\-json
.EE
.RE
.PP
Switch the compressor on for listening at night, try the chain on a file
written to a WAV file, and play an audiobook a little faster in the running
application:
.PP
.RS
.EX
helikodu\-cli dsp compressor night
helikodu\-cli play \-\-dsp \-\-backend wav \-\-wav\-out out.wav track.flac
helikodu\-cli speed 1.25
.EE
.RE
